esp32s31: give Linux the microSD's power switch

GPIO39 is BSP_SD_CTRL in Espressif's own board support - "active-low
power/switch control" - and Linux neither knew about it nor drove it. Measured
before wiring: GPIO_ENABLE1 bit 7 was 0, so the pad was not driven at all and
floated low, which happens to mean "on". The card was therefore permanently
powered and could never be cycled.

That is why a wedged card was unrecoverable. esp_restart() resets the SoC and
not the card, so a card interrupted mid-write keeps holding DAT0 low, the first
CMD0 times out against it, and no reset available to software could clear it -
only pulling the board's power did.

With a fixed regulator on that GPIO and vmmc-supply on the host, the MMC core
cycles the card as part of ordinary initialisation.

**It recovered a wedged card in practice.** The board had been stuck on the
flash rescue root across several EN resets and a Linux reboot; flashing this
brought the card back on the next boot with no physical repower:

	mmcblk0: mmc0:d555 SK128 119 GiB
	root: microSD after 0s
	/ is /dev/mmcblk0 on / type ext4 (rw,relatime)

Worth recording that a *manual* toggle of the same pin from the rescue shell -
enable the output, drive high ~4 s, drive low - did NOT revive it, even though
GPIO_IN1 bit 7 confirmed the pin moved. Doing it through the regulator, as part
of the MMC core's own power-up sequence, did. So the timing and ordering the
core applies matter; poking the GPIO by hand is not equivalent.

Needs CONFIG_REGULATOR and CONFIG_REGULATOR_FIXED_VOLTAGE, which were not set -
see the Makefile change alongside this.

--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/esp32s31_generic.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/esp32s31_generic.dts
@@ -36,8 +36,41 @@
  * could load an overlay. These pins are shared with the module's SPI NAND;
  * the board selects microSD by default.
  */
+/*
+ * The card's power switch, which nothing was driving.
+ *
+ * GPIO39 is BSP_SD_CTRL in Espressif's own board support - "active-low
+ * power/switch control", asserted low - and until now Linux neither knew about
+ * it nor drove it, so the pin floated low (permanently on) and the card could
+ * never be power-cycled. That matters because esp_restart() resets the SoC and
+ * not the card: a card interrupted mid-write holds DAT0 low, the first CMD0
+ * times out against it, and nothing short of pulling the board's power clears
+ * it. With a regulator here the MMC core cycles the card as part of normal
+ * initialisation.
+ *
+ * Measured before wiring it: ENABLE1 bit 7 was 0, so the pad was not driven at
+ * all, and toggling it does move the pin (GPIO_IN1 bit 7 follows). What is NOT
+ * established is that a cycle here clears an already-wedged card - one attempt
+ * with ~4 s off did not revive one. It should stop the state arising, which is
+ * the more useful half.
+ */
+/ {
+	sd_pwr: regulator-sd-vcc {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "sd-vcc";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
+		gpio = <&gpio 39 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		/* No enable-active-high: the switch asserts LOW, per the BSP. */
+		regulator-boot-on;
+		/* SD spec wants the rail stable before the first command. */
+		startup-delay-us = <20000>;
+	};
+};
+
 &sdmmc {
 	status = "okay";
+	vmmc-supply = <&sd_pwr>;
 };
 
 / {
